#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:7
msgid "gitformat-signature - Git cryptographic signature formats"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:13
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<[tag|commit] object header(s)>\n"
"<over-the-wire protocol>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:15
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:23
msgid ""
"Git uses cryptographic signatures in various places, currently objects "
"(tags, commits, mergetags) and transactions (pushes). In every case, the "
"command which is about to create an object or transaction determines a "
"payload from that, calls an external program to obtain a detached signature "
"for the payload (`gpg -bsa` in the case of PGP signatures), and embeds the "
"signature into the object or transaction."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:27
msgid ""
"Signatures begin with an \"ASCII Armor\" header line and end with a tail "
"line, which differ depending on signature type (as selected by `gpg.format`, "
"see linkgit:git-config[1]). These are, for `gpg.format` values:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:28
#, no-wrap
msgid "`gpg` (PGP)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:32
msgid ""
"`-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----` and `-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----`.  Or, if "
"gpg is told to produce RFC1991 signatures, `-----BEGIN PGP MESSAGE-----` and "
"`-----END PGP MESSAGE-----`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "`ssh` (SSH)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:35
msgid "`-----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----` and `-----END SSH SIGNATURE-----`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:36
#, no-wrap
msgid "`x509` (X.509)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:38
msgid "`-----BEGIN SIGNED MESSAGE-----` and `-----END SIGNED MESSAGE-----`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:48
msgid ""
"Signatures sometimes appear as a part of the normal payload (e.g. a signed "
"tag has the signature block appended after the payload that the signature "
"applies to), and sometimes appear in the value of an object header (e.g. a "
"merge commit that merged a signed tag would have the entire tag contents on "
"its \"mergetag\" header).  In the case of the latter, the usual multi-line "
"formatting rule for object headers applies.  I.e. the second and subsequent "
"lines are prefixed with a SP to signal that the line is continued from the "
"previous line."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:54
msgid ""
"This is even true for an originally empty line.  In the following examples, "
"the end of line that ends with a whitespace letter is highlighted with a `$` "
"sign; if you are trying to recreate these example by hand, do not cut and "
"paste them--they are there primarily to highlight extra whitespace at the "
"end of some lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation/gitformat-signature.txt:57
msgid ""
"The signed payload and the way the signature is embedded depends on the type "
"of the object resp. transaction."
msgstr ""

